The app built from ui/ (into web/app/, desktop/build/ui/ or phone/dist/) holds the code of npm packages, each under its
own license. Each build writes THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt beside the app, naming every package the build holds, with its
version, its license and the license files it ships, and puts this LICENSE, LICENSE-APACHE-2.0 and NOTICE beside it
(ui/licenses.ts). A package the app bundles carries one of the permissive licenses ui/licenses.ts allows, and a license
file: its own or, for one that ships none, its project's own license file, kept in ui/third-party/ from its repository,
as the notice says; anything else stops the build (ui/licenses.ts), and ui/src/licenses.test.ts builds the app and holds
it to that. The app's Licenses dialog (in its account menu, its palette and on its sign-in screen) shows these files
and, in the desktop and phone apps, those below.

The desktop app (desktop/) also carries a Python and the Rust crates its shell is built from, and the phone app (phone/)
Rust crates, each under its own license; their builds write the notices into them (desktop/licenses.py). The desktop
app's Resources/licenses/ holds this LICENSE, LICENSE-APACHE-2.0 and NOTICE, the page's notice as
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES-page.txt, THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES-python.txt and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES-rust.txt, with the Rust standard
library's notices as the toolchain that builds the app ships them (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES-rust-std.html); the last three
are beside the window's pages too, and the phone app carries the two Rust ones beside its pages.

THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES-python.txt names CPython, with its license, its acknowledgements and the notices of what it
incorporates, from its own source, and a summary of the changes made to it, which its license asks for; the libraries
python-build-standalone links into it; each Python package the app installs, and each component its SBOMs list as
built into it; and, on an Intel Mac, the OpenSSL built for cryptography. THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES-rust.txt names each crate
the shell links for the machine's architecture, as cargo's resolver builds it.

Each is named with its license and license files: its own or, for one that ships none, those of a crate published from
its repository under the same license, the text of a license it offers that names no holder, or its repository's own
license files at the commit it was published from (desktop/third-party/rust/); the notice says which, and names no
holder its project does not. The crates objc2, block2, objc2-encode and objc2-foundation are given their repository's
own LICENSE.md, kept in desktop/third-party/rust/madsmtm/objc2/; it names no copyright holder, and says that the crates
from that repository (the desktop app carries 11 of them, the phone app 16) are derived from Apple's SDKs and that it is
unclear whether Xcode's license allows such derived works to be distributed. The desktop app depends on objc2 itself,
and tauri, wry, tao and muda, among others, bring it in too; in the phone app, tauri, wry and tao bring it in. Each
carries a permissive license desktop/licenses.py allows, whose conditions for software carried in a built app the
notices meet (the copyright notice and license text as its project gives them, an Apache NOTICE file among the license
files where one is shipped, and for Python the summary of changes), or, for a Rust crate from crates.io, the Mozilla
Public License 2.0, with where its source is there; anything else, or anything with no license text, stops the build.
desktop/third-party/ holds license texts as their projects publish them: CPython's, from its source, and those
python-build-standalone ships for the libraries it links in (python/), crates' repositories' (rust/), and the Mozilla
Public License 2.0 as cssparser 0.37.0 ships it. tests/test_desktop_licenses.py holds the builds to all this.

Its tests include three files of test vectors from Project Wycheproof (tests/vectors/wycheproof/, from
https://github.com/C2SP/wycheproof, testvectors_v1, unchanged), under the Apache License, Version 2.0, whose text is
LICENSE-APACHE-2.0 beside this one.

Its tests also carry, from others:
- tests/vectors/xwing_draft11.json: X-Wing's test vectors, copied from draft-connolly-cfrg-xwing-kem-11, Appendix C
  (https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-connolly-cfrg-xwing-kem-11.txt), under the terms that document states; the
  file names its source and the sha256 of the text it was taken from.
- tests/test_ade_push.py: values from RFC 8291's example (https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8291), under the terms
  that RFC states.
- tests/fixtures/mcp_registry_filesystem.json: a page of the Model Context Protocol registry's list of servers, with
  the descriptions their publishers gave them, which are their publishers' and to which the license above does not
  extend.
